  <Slide id="why-not-temporal" title="Why not just Temporal?" minutes={0.75} place="greenwich">
    <p class="gmt-lead">Temporal is the right engine. Three things it leaves to you:</p>
    <ol class="gmt-why">
      <li><strong>Bad input throws.</strong> Every call needs a try/catch. GMT returns an empty string you check once.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>A time that never happened is moved, not refused.</strong> 03:30 in Helsinki on 28 March 2027 quietly
        becomes 04:30. GMT can say it does not exist.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Engines and polyfills disagree.</strong> GMT works around the bugs that are still open, tests each
        workaround in {gmtStats.timezones} time zones, and removes it when the fix ships.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <Notes>
      <p><strong>On screen:</strong> three things Temporal leaves to you.</p>
      <p><strong>Say:</strong> "Temporal is the right engine, but it is strict in the wrong places. A bad string throws a RangeError, so every call needs a try/catch. And a time that never happened is quietly moved forward: that is the default, <code>disambiguation: "compatible"</code>. Those two are design choices. The third is bugs: engines and polyfills give different answers. The next slide answers the Helsinki question."</p>
      <p>
        Links if asked: <Link href={pageHref("/upstream/")}>every bug we filed</Link>, <Link href={pageHref("/pulse/")}>the weekly
        check</Link> and <Link href="https://github.com/northguild/gmt/issues/316">issue #316</Link>.
      </p>
    </Notes>
  </Slide>

  <Slide id="gmt" title="GMT" minutes={1} place="greenwich">
    <p class="gmt-lead">GMT has {gmtStats.functions} functions with familiar names. GMT worked out every result below.</p>
    <GmtRun
      calls={[
        { module: "instant/convert", name: "resolveLocal", args: ["2027-03-28T03:30:00", "Europe/Helsinki", { disambiguation: "reject" }] },
        { module: "instant/convert", name: "classifyLocal", args: ["2027-03-28T03:30:00", "Europe/Helsinki"] },
      ]}
    >
      <p class="gmt-run-note" data-after>That time does not exist in Helsinki: the clocks skip 03:00 to 04:00.</p>
    </GmtRun>
    <Build n={1} clockAt={gapBefore} />
    <Build n={2} clockAt={gapAfter}>
      <p class="gmt-lead">One minute later the clocks have skipped an hour.</p>
    </Build>
    <Notes>
      <p><strong>On screen:</strong> two GMT calls on the question from the start: 03:30 in Helsinki on 28 March 2027.</p>
      <p><strong>Say:</strong> "Here is the agents' question, answered. The first call asks for a time that never happened in Helsinki. GMT does not guess. It refuses. The second call says what kind of time it is: nonexistent."</p>
      <p><strong>Press →:</strong> the clock takes the Earth's place, at 02:59 in Helsinki on 28 March 2027.</p>
      <p><strong>Press →:</strong> one minute later. The clock reads 04:00, and the offset under the city changes from UTC+02:00 to UTC+03:00.</p>
    </Notes>
  </Slide>

  <Slide id="familiar" title="Names you already know" minutes={1} place="greenwich" layout="familiar">
